  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nThe stretch of dry, low-impact weather will span into next week\nas the central U.S. remains under the influence of a blocking \npattern, which is only forecast to amplify through the weekend.\n\nThe net effect will be ideal mid-autumn weather with daily highs\nin the 70s and overnight lows in the 40s. For those keeping \nscore at home, these temperatures are averaging about 8 degrees \nwarmer than what is considered normal for middle October. While\nnot record- warmth by any means, it is nevertheless impressive.\n\nMid-range deterministic model guidance demonstrates strong\nconsistency regarding the progression of the broad western\ntrough into early next week. While successive model cycles\ncontinue to open and eject this feature northeastward through\nthe Central Plains, the attendant cold front consistently fails \nto push through central Illinois. Rather, the surface low \nundergoes occlusion over the Canadian Prairies, causing the \ntrailing front to stall and weaken over the Great Lakes before \neventually washing out and retreating north as a warm front.\n\nIt's not until Wednesday that a strong mid-level shortwave\ntrough pivots across the Mid-Mississippi Valley and drives a\nsurface cold front through the Midwest and Great Lakes region,\neffectively breaking down our resident ridge. This could be our\nnext best chance for scattered showers and isolated\nthunderstorms, though current blended (NBM) and ensemble \nguidance (GEFS/EPS) mute the probability at 20% or less.",
